      "claim": "An idempotent operation guarantees the resulting system state is identical after one or multiple executions (f(f(x)) = f(x)), whereas a safe operation merely guarantees no harmful side effects or corruption on retry, potentially leaving the system in an altered, though benign, state. To survive at-least-once delivery and ensure deterministic replay, an append-only event log fundamentally requires its writers to be idempotent, either inherently or by enforcing content-defined deduplication.",
      "mechanism": "At-least-once delivery means a writer's request may be executed multiple times. If the operation is merely safe (e.g., appending a random UUID), the log accumulates multiple distinct entries, violating deterministic replay. Idempotency ensures that repeated appends of the exact same event yield a single logical entry, making the replayed state perfectly match the primary path.",
